      "observed_sha256": "b8d5786a01c4c2e49537d20b54df7dcfec09db35afab4a4681fc3d407138f24f"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:11:2",
      "id": "thm-levi-decomposition-of-standard-parabolics-in-gl-n-fq",
      "location": "Proof step 1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof says that if A_{ii}v=0, “the block column computation ... shows that p annihilates” the vector supported on block I_i. An upper block-triangular column also has entries in earlier blocks: for α=(1,1), p=[[1,1],[0,0]] and v=1 in block 2 give A_{22}v=0 but p(0,v)=(1,0)≠0. This does not establish injectivity of A_{ii}; block-triangular determinant or elimination would close the gap.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "26bd1dc3a8d745b72a7dbe703202200838b617c48100a2285acc5b07064628a8"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:11:3",
      "id": "thm-relative-position-classifies-pairs-of-complete-flags",
      "location": "Proof step 4.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The step says the dimensions determine the “whole southwest rank matrix” because they determine r_{i+1,j} for all i,j and r_{n+1,j}=0. Those data give rows 2 through n and the zero row, but not row 1. Since x=h^{-1}g and x'=h'^{-1}g' are invertible, r_{1,j}(x)=r_{1,j}(x')=j; stating this fact completes the inference.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "6b4a207985d2820538a0da773b9b68c538adbdcff9250170433511b4cf0ffd8c"
    }
